## Buying a Single Stock Like SpaceX (SPCX) for Your Child

You can buy SpaceX stock for your child by purchasing whole or fractional shares of SPCX inside a custodial brokerage account, though a Trump Account cannot hold it because families may only buy its index fund. Federal rules restrict Trump Account family contributions strictly to broad index funds tracking the S&P 500 or similar equities. Opening a standard custodial brokerage account under the rules in this guide removes that limitation, allowing you to execute single-stock trades as detailed in our guide on [how to buy SpaceX stock](/guides/how-to-buy-spacex-stock/).

Log in to the custodial account and search for SPCX on Nasdaq. SpaceX priced its initial public offering on June 12, 2026, selling 555,555,555 Class A shares at $135 each, and closed at $146.03 on October 16, 2026, within a 52-week range of $104.83 to $225.64. Because share prices change every trading day, check a live quote on [Stock Analysis](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/spcx/) and use a [limit order](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/limit-order) to set your maximum purchase price, selecting [fractional share investing](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/fractional-share-investing) if your broker allows purchasing part of a share.

A planned giveaway may offer another path. On October 19, 2026, Elon Musk posted on X that he plans to give one of his SpaceX shares to every American child next year, a pledge that would total roughly $10.5 billion to $11 billion depending on the share price used. Musk did not establish a claim website, application form, or delivery rules, so review our report on [Elon Musk's SpaceX share for kids](/guides/elon-musk-spacex-share-for-kids/) to track verified program details.

If you purchase SPCX directly for your child, keep the allocation small. Placing a large portion of a child's portfolio into any single stock exposes their savings to [concentrated stock position risk](/guides/concentrated-stock-position-risk/), where company difficulties can cause sharp losses. Buying one share or a fractional slice works well as a keepsake or an introduction to investing, provided the foundation of their portfolio stays in broad index funds.